  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note-001",
      "author": "registry_admin",
      "date": "2025-03-01T14:22:00Z",
      "category": "verification",
      "text": "Identity verified against civil registry. All documents checked."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-002",
      "author": "miriam.cohen",
      "date": "2026-01-18T22:10:00Z",
      "category": "personal",
      "text": "Finished Klara and the Sun last night. One of the most affecting things I have read in years — the narrator's voice stayed with me in a way I didn't expect. Ishiguro somehow made me feel the limits of perception more viscerally than any paper I've read on the subject."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-003",
      "author": "miriam.cohen",
      "date": "2025-11-28T09:15:00Z",
      "category": "personal",
      "text": "Back into Beethoven's late string quartets this week — Op. 131 specifically. There's a structural patience to it that I find completely settling during long writing sessions. Also been listening to Noa Kirel, which my students find very funny."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-004",
      "author": "miriam.cohen",
      "date": "2025-09-04T08:40:00Z",
      "category": "personal",
      "text": "Found a coffee place in Florentin — Café Levinsky 41. Tiny, neighbourhood feel, excellent pour-over. Ten-minute walk from home and it's already becoming a morning ritual. Also finally tried HaBasta properly for a long Friday lunch — seasonal menu, outstanding."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-005",
      "author": "miriam.cohen",
      "date": "2026-02-14T21:30:00Z",
      "category": "personal",
      "text": "Someone at the department asked if I believe in astrology. I find the question genuinely interesting. I don't think the positions of celestial bodies at my birth shaped my personality — but I do think people use zodiac descriptions as a mirror, and mirrors can be useful even when they're approximate. I'm a Gemini, apparently. The duality thing doesn't feel entirely wrong. The Ox in the Chinese calendar — stubborn, hardworking, private — fits better than I'd like to admit."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-006",
      "author": "miriam.cohen",
      "date": "2025-10-03T08:55:00Z",
      "category": "personal",
      "text": "If I could go anywhere I haven't been: Kyoto. Specifically in autumn. There's a kind of deliberateness to how the city is designed — the spatial logic of the temples, the way noise is managed, the relationship between inside and outside — that I want to experience directly. I've read too much about it. Time to actually go."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-007",
      "author": "miriam.cohen",
      "date": "2025-06-20T10:10:00Z",
      "category": "personal",
      "text": "Friday morning ritual: up at 6:30, walk to the Carmel Market alone before it fills up. Buy whatever looks good — no list, no plan. Home by 8:30, cook something slow for Shabbat. I've been doing this since we moved to Rothschild in 2018 and it's the one weekly thing that feels entirely mine."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-008",
      "author": "miriam.cohen",
      "date": "2024-11-09T16:45:00Z",
      "category": "personal",
      "text": "Picked up another Philip K. Dick first edition at the Jaffa flea market — 'Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep?', 1968 Doubleday, decent condition. The spine has a crack but the dust jacket is intact. Now up to eleven in the collection. There's something satisfying about owning a physical object that was printed before I was born and still thinking through the same questions."
    }
  ],
